.TH BC250\-BIOS\-COMPARE\-CLI 1 "2026-08-30" "bc250-bios-compare 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bc250\-bios\-compare\-cli \- read BC\-250 BIOS menus from the command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bc250\-bios\-compare\-cli volumes
.I IMAGE
.br
.B bc250\-bios\-compare\-cli formsets
.I IMAGE
.br
.B bc250\-bios\-compare\-cli tree
.I IMAGE
.RB [ \-\-formset
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-all ]
.RB [ \-\-variable
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B bc250\-bios\-compare\-cli entry
.I IMAGE TEXT
.RB [ \-\-variable
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B bc250\-bios\-compare\-cli simulate
.I IMAGE
.IR ENTRY = VALUE ...
.br
.B bc250\-bios\-compare\-cli compare
.I IMAGE\-A IMAGE\-B
.br
.B bc250\-bios\-compare\-cli export
.I IMAGE FILE.json
.SH DESCRIPTION
The same engine as the window, without the window: it needs no display and is
what to use over ssh.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B volumes
The UEFI volumes found in the image, by the \fB_FVH\fR signature, with the
header checksum validated.
.TP
.B formsets
The form sets: how many entries each holds, how many of those carry a
condition that can hide them, and which variable they are stored in.
.TP
.B tree
The menus, following the \fBRef\fR entries \- which is where the hierarchy
really is, since inside a form set all forms are siblings. Forms no link
leads to are listed apart.
.TP
.B entry
Everything about one entry: where its value is stored (variable, GUID,
offset), the options, the defaults, and the conditions with their outcome.
.TP
.B simulate
What changes when entries are set to a value: which other entries appear and
which disappear.
.TP
.B compare
The differences between the menus of two images \- the menus, not the bytes.
This is how a modified BIOS is told from the stock one.
.TP
.B export
Everything, as JSON.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-formset " N"
Which form set to work on; without it, the one with the entries.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Include the entries the firmware hides, marked rather than left out.
.TP
.BI \-\-variable " FILE"
Read the values from a variable dumped off a running board instead of the
firmware defaults, so the menu comes out as it is right now on that board.
.SH READING THE VARIABLE OFF A BOARD
.B AmdSetup
is visible from Linux on a running board, but
.B scp will not copy it
\- efivarfs does not allow that. It has to go through base64:
.PP
.RS
ssh root@board "base64 \-w0 /sys/firmware/efi/efivars/AmdSetup\-3a997502\-647a\-4c82\-998e\-52ef9486a247" | base64 \-d > AmdSetup.bin
.RE
.PP
The first four bytes of that file are the attributes and not part of the
variable; they are dropped after the length is checked.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Done.
.TP
.B 1
The image could not be read, or the request made no sense for it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bc250\-bios\-compare (1)
.SH AUTHOR
MTSistemi.
